#
# ~/.config/wardyn/runnerd.env — read by the wardyn-runnerd systemd user unit.
# `wardyn-runnerd setup` (and deploy/runner/install.sh, which runs it) writes this file (mode 0600); this is its shape.

# The organisation's public URL, exactly as it was at registration.
WARDYN_RUNNER_ORG_URL=https://wardyn.example
# The proxy image. setup writes the image the organisation named at registration. An unmarked image
# follows the organisation: serve takes the image it answers at each connect. A pin is the image below
# marked WARDYN_PROXY_IMAGE_PINNED=1, or a mirror that is neither the registration image nor this
# project's release image. Left out, serve uses the registration image, then the release image.
# WARDYN_PROXY_IMAGE=ghcr.io/cjohnstoniv/wardyn-proxy:0.9.0
# Written beside an image --proxy-image named: serve then never replaces it, and setup run again keeps it.
# WARDYN_PROXY_IMAGE_PINNED=1
# The local Docker bridge that proxy sidecars attach to (serve creates it when missing).
WARDYN_INTERNAL_NETWORK=wardyn-internal
# Optional: the Docker socket, only when it is not the default (rootless Docker, Rancher Desktop, Colima).
# DOCKER_HOST=unix:///run/user/1000/docker.sock
# Where the run relay listens, and what a run's proxy reaches it at (host-gateway or an IP). setup writes
# both on a VM-backed engine (Docker Desktop, Rancher Desktop, Colima): the pair its probe container
# reached. A native Linux engine needs neither. Set them only to override what setup found.
# WARDYN_RELAY_LISTEN=127.0.0.1
# WARDYN_RELAY_HOST_TARGET=host-gateway
# Optional: a PEM bundle of your organisation's extra roots (a TLS-inspecting proxy's CA), trusted by
# every run's proxy and sandbox beside the system roots. setup --trusted-ca-file writes it.
# WARDYN_TRUSTED_CA_FILE=/home/me/.config/wardyn/corp-ca.pem
# Optional: a PEM file of extra roots that runnerd's own connection to the organisation trusts, registration
# included, and nothing else (no run's proxy or sandbox gets it). Not writable by group or others; owned by
# you or root. setup --org-ca-file writes it.
# WARDYN_ORG_CA_FILE=/home/me/.config/wardyn/org-ca.pem
# Optional: the proxy for the runner's own connection to the organisation. setup copies HTTPS_PROXY,
# HTTP_PROXY and NO_PROXY from your shell with any user name and password removed, and sets no NO_PROXY
# default. A proxy that needs a password refuses the runner; Wardyn does not store it.
# HTTPS_PROXY=http://proxy.example:3128
